        summary: 'SSRIs occupy the serotonin transporter (SERT) that MDMA relies on, significantly blunting effects. A 2022 systematic review found this interaction reduces, not increases, direct serotonin syndrome risk, but creates a dangerous trap: reduced euphoria drives redosing, which sharply raises cardiovascular and overdose risk <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9177763/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="supp-link">[PMC9177763]</a>.',
        mechanism: 'SSRIs chronically downregulate SERT, reducing MDMA\'s ability to flood serotonin into synapses. However, MDMA\'s cardiovascular effects (heart rate, blood pressure, hyperthermia) persist independently of SERT occupancy. SNRIs add norepinephrine reuptake inhibition, further straining the heart.',
        advice: 'Avoid. The reduced euphoria almost always leads to dangerous redosing. Cardiovascular risk, the leading cause of MDMA-related acute harm, is unchanged by SSRI co-administration. Discuss any SSRI changes with your prescribing doctor.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('mdma','cocaine')]: {
        severity: 'caution',
        label: 'HIGH RISK, Cardiovascular & metabolic danger',
        title: 'MDMA + Cocaine',
        summary: 'Cocaine inhibits CYP2D6, the liver enzyme responsible for metabolizing MDMA. This causes unpredictable MDMA blood-level spikes. Both substances independently strain the cardiovascular system; combined the risk is substantial.',
        mechanism: 'CYP2D6 inhibition by cocaine slows MDMA clearance, raising plasma MDMA concentrations beyond expected levels. Additive stimulant burden elevates heart rate, blood pressure, and body temperature, all major factors in MDMA-related acute harm.',
        advice: 'Avoid combining. If both are used, reduce doses of each significantly, maintain a cool environment, and have a sober person present who can call for help.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('mdma','lithium')]: {
        severity: 'caution',
        label: 'HIGH RISK, Serotonin syndrome & lithium toxicity',
        title: 'MDMA + Lithium',
        summary: 'Lithium potentiates serotonergic activity, increasing serotonin syndrome risk when combined with MDMA. Additionally, MDMA-related dehydration and overheating can concentrate lithium to toxic serum levels.',
        mechanism: 'Lithium augments serotonin neurotransmission through multiple pathways. Combined with MDMA\'s massive serotonin release, this raises serotonin syndrome risk. Fluid loss from MDMA reduces lithium clearance, potentially causing lithium toxicity.',
        advice: 'Do not combine. If on lithium for bipolar disorder, MDMA is contraindicated. Lithium toxicity symptoms (tremor, confusion, nausea) can be confused with MDMA side effects, masking a serious medical emergency.',
