        title: 'Cocaine + Alcohol',
        summary: 'The most common combination in cocaine-related cardiac deaths. Alcohol and cocaine combine in the liver to form cocaethylene, a compound more cardiotoxic and longer-lasting than cocaine alone. A 2024 systematic review found cocaethylene carries an 18–25x increased risk of sudden death vs. cocaine alone <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38592322/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="supp-link">[PMID 38592322]</a>; a 2023 emergency department study confirmed cocaethylene-associated myocardial injury <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36000306/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="supp-link">[PMID 36000306]</a>.',
        mechanism: 'Liver transesterification of cocaine in the presence of ethanol produces cocaethylene. This compound is more toxic to the heart than cocaine, has a ~5-hour half-life (vs. ~1 hour for cocaine), and sustains cardiovascular stress for hours after cocaine has cleared. Production is dose-dependent on alcohol intake.',
        advice: 'Avoid. If using cocaine socially where alcohol is present, minimize alcohol intake drastically. There is no safe amount of this combination, only less dangerous amounts.',
        emergency: true
      },
