      [key('ghb','alcohol')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, Respiratory depression & coma',
        title: 'GHB / GBL + Alcohol',
        summary: 'One of the most dangerous combinations at raves and festivals. Both GHB and alcohol are CNS depressants acting on overlapping GABA systems. Co-administration produces synergistic (not merely additive) respiratory depression, with multiple documented deaths. Clinical toxicology reviews identify alcohol as the factor most responsible for converting GHB intoxication into fatality <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2766839/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="supp-link">[PMC2766839]</a>.',
        mechanism: 'GHB acts on GABA-B and its own GHB receptors to produce CNS depression. Alcohol potentiates GABA-A and inhibits NMDA, adding to the depressant load via different receptor populations. The combined effect on brainstem respiratory centers can be lethal even at individually sub-lethal doses.',
        advice: 'Do not combine. GHB has a notoriously steep dose-response curve, the margin between euphoria and unconsciousness is narrow. Alcohol shifts this curve dangerously. If someone is unresponsive after GHB + alcohol, place in recovery position immediately and call emergency services.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('benzo','ghb')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, Respiratory depression',
        title: 'GHB / GBL + Benzodiazepines',
        summary: 'Like GHB and alcohol, this combination creates synergistic CNS depression with real risk of respiratory arrest. GHB\'s steep dose-response curve means even a modest benzo dose can push GHB from sedating to lethal.',
        mechanism: 'GABA-A potentiation from benzos combined with GHB\'s GABA-B agonism and own-receptor activity multiply brainstem depression. Onset timing mismatch (benzos are slower) means the full combined effect often becomes apparent only after both have been absorbed.',
        advice: 'Do not combine. If someone who has taken both becomes unresponsive, call emergency services and place in recovery position. Monitor breathing continuously.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('ghb','ketamine')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, CNS depression & respiratory arrest',
        title: 'GHB / GBL + Ketamine',
        summary: 'Both are CNS depressants with dissociative properties. Combined, they can cause profound unconsciousness and respiratory arrest. Neither substance alone reliably presents the warning signs of declining consciousness that would prompt intervention.',
        mechanism: 'GHB\'s GABA-B agonism combined with ketamine\'s NMDA antagonism and opioid activity stack depressant effects across multiple receptor systems. The k-hole combined with GHB-induced unconsciousness removes all ability to signal distress.',
        advice: 'Avoid entirely. This combination requires a fully sober monitor. If someone goes unresponsive, call emergency services immediately and place in recovery position.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('ghb','mdma')]: {
