      [key('alcohol','ssri')]: {
        severity: 'low',
        label: 'LOW–MODERATE, Impaired judgment & reduced alcohol tolerance',
        title: 'Alcohol + SSRIs / SNRIs',
        summary: 'SSRIs/SNRIs can reduce alcohol tolerance and increase its sedative effects in some people. The combination is common but warrants caution, especially in the first weeks of starting an SSRI.',
        mechanism: 'Some SSRIs inhibit CYP2E1, an enzyme involved in alcohol metabolism, potentially raising blood alcohol levels. CNS depressant effects may be additive in sensitive individuals.',
        advice: 'If on SSRIs, be aware your alcohol tolerance may be lower than usual. Start with a smaller amount and assess your response. Avoid excessive drinking, many SSRIs are hepatically metabolized and chronic heavy drinking worsens their efficacy.',
        emergency: false
      },

      [key('cannabis','cocaine')]: {
        severity: 'low',
        label: 'LOW–MODERATE, Cardiovascular strain',
        title: 'Cannabis + Cocaine',
        summary: 'THC raises heart rate; cocaine raises heart rate and blood pressure dramatically. Combined, the cardiovascular burden is meaningfully higher. Anxiety and paranoia are also more common.',
        mechanism: 'CB1 activation by THC elevates resting heart rate. Cocaine\'s catecholamine effects add to this. In people with existing cardiovascular sensitivity, the combination can trigger arrhythmias.',
        advice: 'Limit cannabis use when using cocaine. Avoid concentrates or high-THC products. Be attentive to chest discomfort, it warrants stopping both substances and seeking medical assessment.',
