        label: 'LOW RISK, Slowed nicotine metabolism',
        title: 'Nicotine + MAOIs',
        summary: 'MAO-B is involved in nicotine metabolism. MAOIs slow nicotine breakdown, which is one reason some MAOIs have been studied for smoking cessation. This is a mild interaction relevant mainly at high nicotine doses or with irreversible MAOIs.',
        mechanism: 'Nicotine is metabolized primarily by CYP2A6, with MAO-B contributing. MAOI inhibition of MAO-B slows a secondary nicotine metabolic pathway, mildly increasing nicotine exposure per cigarette or vape session.',
        advice: 'If on prescribed MAOIs and smoking or vaping, nicotine effects may be slightly stronger than usual. No specific action required at normal nicotine doses, but avoid unusually high nicotine intake.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('nicotine','ketamine')]: {
        severity: 'low',
        label: 'LOW RISK, Minor interaction',
        title: 'Nicotine + Ketamine',
        summary: 'No significant direct pharmacological interaction. Nicotine adds mild cardiovascular stimulation; ketamine adds dissociative cardiovascular sympathomimetic effects. Both elevate heart rate and blood pressure mildly.',
        mechanism: 'Nicotinic receptor stimulation by nicotine causes mild catecholamine release. Ketamine\'s sympathomimetic effects are primarily NMDA-related. No significant pharmacokinetic interaction.',
        advice: 'The main practical concern is smoking or vaping while in a ketamine-altered state — coordination impairment increases burn and accident risk. Use caution if combining.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('nicotine','ghb')]: {
        severity: 'low',
        label: 'LOW RISK, Minor interaction',
        title: 'Nicotine + GHB / GBL',
        summary: 'No significant direct pharmacological interaction between nicotine and GHB. Nicotine\'s stimulant effects may partially mask GHB\'s sedation, making it slightly harder to gauge intoxication depth.',
        mechanism: 'GHB\'s CNS depressant effects (GABA-B agonism) and nicotine\'s mild stimulant effects work in opposing directions at the receptor level without significant pharmacokinetic interaction.',
        advice: 'The masking concern is the practical issue: nicotine\'s stimulation can make GHB sedation less apparent, leading to accidental redosing of GHB. Dose GHB conservatively and avoid relying on how alert you feel.',
