      // — DMT —
      [key('dmt','maoi')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, Serotonin syndrome / massively prolonged experience',
        title: 'DMT + MAOIs',
        summary: 'MAOIs are the pharmacological basis of ayahuasca — harmaline/harmine (reversible MAO-A inhibitors) in the brew prevent gut and liver destruction of oral DMT. With prescription irreversible MAOIs (phenelzine, tranylcypromine), this combination is extremely dangerous: serotonin syndrome risk is high, the duration extends unpredictably to many hours, and the intensity is far beyond what smoked DMT produces.',
        mechanism: 'DMT is orally inactive because gut and liver MAO-A destroys it before it reaches circulation. MAO-A inhibition bypasses this, allowing full oral bioavailability. Combined with DMT\'s 5-HT2A agonism and MAOI-mediated elevation of synaptic serotonin, the serotonergic load can reach dangerous levels. Irreversible MAOIs have a 2-week washout period during which any serotonergic substance is high risk.',
        advice: 'Do not combine DMT with prescription MAOIs. Traditional ayahuasca ceremonies use carefully calibrated reversible MAO-A inhibitors — this is not the same as taking DMT while on prescribed phenelzine or tranylcypromine. If you are on a prescribed MAOI, DMT in any form is contraindicated.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('dmt','mdma')]: {
        severity: 'caution',
        label: 'HIGH RISK, Serotonergic overload',
        title: 'DMT + MDMA',
        summary: 'Both are potent serotonergic compounds. MDMA causes a massive non-vesicular serotonin flood via SERT reversal. DMT is a 5-HT2A agonist that also has some affinity for serotonin transporters. Combined, the serotonergic load elevates serotonin syndrome risk and the cardiovascular burden (hyperthermia, tachycardia) from MDMA is unchanged.',
        mechanism: 'MDMA drives synaptic serotonin to extremely high levels via SERT reversal. DMT activates 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C receptors — which MDMA\'s serotonin flood also overstimulates. The psychedelic intensity of DMT is substantially amplified, and serotonin syndrome diagnostic criteria (clonus, hyperthermia, agitation) overlap with MDMA toxicity, making recognition difficult.',
        advice: 'Avoid this combination. If both are used, do not redose either substance. Have a sober sitter. Know the signs of serotonin syndrome: rapid heartbeat, high temperature, muscle twitching, agitation. Seek emergency care if these develop.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('dmt','lithium')]: {
        severity: 'caution',
        label: 'HIGH RISK, Seizure risk',
        title: 'DMT + Lithium',
        summary: 'Lithium combined with serotonergic psychedelics (LSD, psilocybin) is documented to cause seizures and cardiac arrhythmia in multiple case reports. The same mechanism applies to DMT: lithium potentiates serotonergic activity and lowers seizure threshold, creating serious risk when combined with any 5-HT2A agonist.',
        mechanism: 'Lithium augments serotonin neurotransmission and modulates glutamate signaling. Combined with DMT\'s 5-HT2A agonism, it can produce excessive neuronal excitability. Case reports of LSD+lithium seizures (the closest analogue) include grand mal seizures with cardiac complications.',
        advice: 'Do not combine DMT with lithium. If you are prescribed lithium for bipolar disorder, all serotonergic psychedelics including DMT are contraindicated. Discuss any psychedelic use with your prescriber.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('dmt','ssri')]: {
        severity: 'moderate',
        label: 'MODERATE, Blunted effects + serotonin concern',
        title: 'DMT + SSRIs / SNRIs',
        summary: 'Chronic SSRI use downregulates 5-HT2A receptors — the primary target of DMT\'s psychedelic action. Effects will be significantly blunted. Some serotonin syndrome risk exists from combining a direct 5-HT2A agonist with a serotonin reuptake inhibitor, particularly at higher DMT doses or with SNRIs.',
        mechanism: 'SSRIs chronically reduce 5-HT2A receptor density through downregulation. DMT\'s psychedelic effect depends on 5-HT2A activation, so fewer receptors means less effect. However, SSRIs also elevate synaptic serotonin — adding DMT\'s serotonergic activity on top raises the overall serotonergic tone.',
        advice: 'Blunted effects may tempt redosing — this increases risk without reliably restoring the experience. The serotonin concern is real at high doses. Do not combine SNRIs (which also block norepinephrine reuptake) with DMT without understanding the elevated risk profile.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('dmt','psilocybin')]: {
        severity: 'moderate',
        label: 'MODERATE, Intensified psychedelic experience',
        title: 'DMT + Psilocybin',
        summary: 'Both act primarily at 5-HT2A receptors. Combining them produces a dramatically intensified psychedelic experience with largely unpredictable character. No specific dangerous pharmacological interaction beyond additive serotonergic load, but psychological overwhelm and loss of ability to signal distress is the primary risk.',
        mechanism: 'Psilocin (the active metabolite of psilocybin) and DMT both agonize 5-HT2A. Additive receptor activation produces a more intense experience than either alone. Psilocybin\'s 4–6 hour duration means DMT is added on top of an ongoing psychedelic state.',
        advice: 'If combining, use significantly reduced doses of each. Have an experienced, sober sitter. Ensure a controlled setting. The combined experience will be more intense and less predictable than either substance alone — plan accordingly.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('dmt','cannabis')]: {
        severity: 'low',
        label: 'LOW–MODERATE, Intensification and destabilization',
        title: 'DMT + Cannabis',
        summary: 'Cannabis combined with psychedelics reliably intensifies and can destabilize the experience. DMT\'s naturally short and extremely intense window (5–20 min when smoked) is already at the upper limit of what most people can process. Adding cannabis before or during can cause panic, difficulty grounding, and prolonged altered state after DMT clears.',
        mechanism: 'Cannabis (THC) modulates the endocannabinoid system and can potentiate serotonergic receptor signaling. It independently increases sensory intensity and can cause anxiety and paranoia. Combined with DMT\'s complete reality disruption, the risk of a destabilizing experience rises.',
        advice: 'If combining, use cannabis after DMT has fully cleared — not before or during. Cannabis can significantly extend subjective alteration after DMT itself has metabolized. Start with a minimal cannabis amount.',
        emergency: false
      },
      [key('dmt','alcohol')]: {
        severity: 'low',
        label: 'LOW RISK, Reduced experience quality',
        title: 'DMT + Alcohol',
        summary: 'Alcohol blunts psychedelic experiences and is generally considered a poor combination with DMT. No significant dangerous pharmacological interaction, but nausea, impaired coordination during the experience, and reduced set quality are concerns.',
        mechanism: 'Alcohol\'s GABAergic CNS depression partially counteracts the excitatory effects of 5-HT2A agonism. This can flatten the psychedelic experience rather than enhance it. Alcohol also impairs the proprioception and coordination needed to stay safe during an intense dissociative experience.',
        advice: 'Avoid alcohol before DMT. A poor set or impaired coordination during a breakthrough experience increases accident and psychological harm risk. If alcohol has been consumed, wait for it to clear before using DMT.',
        emergency: false
      },

      // — Opioids —
      [key('opioids','alcohol')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, Respiratory depression — leading overdose killer',
        title: 'Opioids + Alcohol',
        summary: 'Opioids and alcohol are among the most common fatal drug combinations. Both suppress the brainstem respiratory centers through different mechanisms, and their effects are synergistic — not merely additive. A blood alcohol level that would not cause harm alone can be enough to cause fatal apnea when combined with an opioid. This combination is present in a substantial proportion of all opioid overdose deaths.',
        mechanism: 'Opioids activate mu-opioid receptors in the brainstem\'s pre-Bötzinger complex, reducing the rate and depth of breathing. Alcohol potentiates GABA-A receptors and inhibits NMDA receptors, independently suppressing respiratory drive. Together, these two mechanisms of respiratory depression compound, pushing breathing toward fatal suppression at doses that might seem moderate individually.',
        advice: 'Do not combine opioids and alcohol. If someone is unresponsive or breathing very slowly after using both, this is a medical emergency — call 911 immediately, administer naloxone (Narcan) if available. Naloxone reverses opioid effects but not alcohol — additional supportive care is needed.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('opioids','benzo')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, FDA black box warning — respiratory failure',
        title: 'Opioids + Benzodiazepines',
        summary: 'The FDA requires a black box warning on all opioid and benzodiazepine prescriptions about this combination. Benzodiazepines are involved in approximately 30% of opioid overdose deaths. Both depress the CNS and respiratory system; the combination is synergistic and can cause respiratory failure at doses of each that might seem individually safe.',
        mechanism: 'Opioids reduce respiratory drive via mu-receptor activation in the brainstem. Benzodiazepines potentiate GABA-A receptors, adding independent CNS and respiratory depression. Together they stack at multiple receptor systems, producing far deeper CNS suppression than either alone. Sleep, already a state of reduced respiratory drive, further amplifies this risk.',
        advice: 'If prescribed both, take exactly as directed. Never increase doses of either. Avoid combining with any additional CNS depressants (alcohol, GHB, sleep aids). Have naloxone accessible — it reverses the opioid component. If someone is unresponsive and breathing slowly or not at all, call 911 and administer naloxone immediately.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('opioids','ketamine')]: {
        severity: 'danger',
        label: 'DANGER, Synergistic CNS and respiratory depression',
        title: 'Opioids + Ketamine',
        summary: 'Ketamine is generally considered a stimulant of the cardiovascular system, but it does have meaningful CNS depressant properties including respiratory depression at higher doses. Combined with opioids, the synergistic respiratory depression risk is clinically significant. In clinical anaesthesia, opioid premedication substantially increases the risk of ketamine-induced apnea.',
        mechanism: 'Ketamine\'s NMDA antagonism causes CNS dissociation and at higher doses suppresses respiratory drive. It also has weak opioid receptor activity. Combined with mu-opioid agonism, CNS depression and respiratory suppression are synergistic. The k-hole, already a state where you cannot call for help, is dramatically more dangerous with opioids present.',
        advice: 'Do not combine ketamine with opioids recreationally. If a medical procedure involves both, this is handled under controlled anaesthetic monitoring. Recreationally, this combination removes the protective effect of consciousness — have naloxone available and never use both alone.',
        emergency: true
      },
      [key('opioids','cocaine')]: {
        severity: 'caution',
        label: 'HIGH RISK, Speedball — masked respiratory depression',
        title: 'Opioids + Cocaine',
        summary: 'The "speedball" — heroin (or other opioids) combined with cocaine — has killed multiple public figures and is documented as one of the most dangerous drug combinations. Cocaine\'s stimulant effects mask the subjective signs of opioid overdose: you feel alert, so you cannot perceive that your breathing is becoming dangerously shallow. When cocaine wears off first, opioid respiratory depression can hit suddenly and fatally.',
        mechanism: 'Cocaine blocks dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin reuptake, producing stimulation and alertness. This masks opioid overdose warning signs (nodding, slowed breathing). Cocaine also has cardiac arrhythmia risk independent of opioids. When cocaine\'s effects end before opioid effects, the unmasked respiratory depression becomes immediately life-threatening.',
